@node Main "openpci-rtl8029.device"

                         openpci-rtl8029.device

  A SANA-II network driver for RTL8029 (NE2000 compatible) PCI Ethernet
  cards on classic AmigaOS, using openpci.library. It works with Roadshow
  and other SANA-II TCP/IP stacks.

     @{" Installation    " link Install}  requirements and how to install
     @{" The driver      " link Driver}  units and the task priority setting
     @{" client          " link Client}  end-to-end network test and benchmark
     @{" s2stat          " link S2stat}  the driver's diagnostic counters
     @{" License         " link License}  copyright, license and source code

@endnode

@node Install "Installation"

  Installation

  The driver needs openpci.library from the openpci package (Aminet
  driver/other/openpci68k.lha), which supports several PCI bridges.

  Double-click the Install icon to run the installer. It checks for the
  card with the lspci command of the openpci package and copies the
  driver to DEVS:Networks/. It can also remove the driver again.

  To install by hand, copy Devs/Networks/openpci-rtl8029.device to
  DEVS:Networks/.

  Then set up an interface for it in your TCP/IP stack. The device name is
  openpci-rtl8029.device and the first card is unit 0. For Roadshow, the
  interface file in DEVS:NetInterfaces/ names them like this:

      device=openpci-rtl8029.device
      unit=0

  The tools client and s2stat are in the C drawer of the distribution.
  They are not needed for normal use.

@endnode

@node Driver "The driver"

  The driver

  Units

  Each RTL8029 card is one unit, numbered from 0 in the order openpci
  finds the cards. Up to 4 cards are supported. A TCP/IP stack that opens
  a unit exclusively, as Roadshow does, has it to itself; @{"s2stat" link S2stat}
  can still look at it.

  Running openpci-rtl8029.device from a Shell does nothing.

  Task priority

  Each unit has a task that does all the packet work. The card can only
  hold about 10 ms of incoming traffic, so this task must get the CPU
  quickly whenever the card has something; otherwise packets are lost.
  It runs at priority 6 by default, above normal programs.

  If packets are lost while the machine is busy, a higher priority can
  help. The environment variable openpci-rtl8029.pri overrides it, from
  -20 to 60. SAVE keeps the setting after a reboot:

      SetEnv SAVE openpci-rtl8029.pri 10

  It is read when a unit starts, so restart the network afterwards (for
  Roadshow: NetShutdown, then AddNetInterface with your interface name).
  Older shells (AmigaOS 3.1) have no SAVE switch: set the variable
  without it, then copy it to ENVARC: yourself:

      Copy ENV:openpci-rtl8029.pri ENVARC:

  @{"s2stat" link S2stat} shows the priority in use as TaskPri.

@endnode

@node Client "client"

  client - end-to-end network test

  Usage:   client SERVERIP/A,BENCH/S

      SERVERIP   address of the computer running the test server
      BENCH      also measure throughput (see below)

  client tests the driver together with a server program on another
  computer: the server is in the source code (src/Tools/macOS), built
  with "make server". Start the server first; it prints its own
  addresses and waits on TCP and UDP port 10203. It serves one run of
  client and then exits, so start it again for every run.

  client then goes through these steps, printing as it goes:

  1. Handshake. "Connected!" and "Received handshake: Hello, Client!".

  2. Receive test. The server sends 14 packets of 64 to 4096 bytes, odd
     and even sizes. For each, client prints the size and either
     "OK: Pattern verified" or the error found. Ends with
     "RX test complete: 14 packets received, 0 errors".

  3. Send test. client sends the same 14 sizes back; each is checked by
     the server, which answers "RX-ACK:n". Ends with "TX test complete".

  4. Byte order test. A 22-byte structure of mixed field sizes goes each
     way and is checked field by field: "PASSED" or "FAILED" for each
     direction.

  5. Frame size test. UDP datagrams that fill exactly one Ethernet frame,
     from the shortest (60 bytes) to the longest (1514 bytes), are echoed
     by the server. Each size is tried up to 3 times and prints:

         OK            the echo came back unchanged
         LOST          no echo within 2 seconds, 3 times
         echo differs  an echo came back damaged

     The TCP steps above cannot catch frames of one size getting lost,
     because TCP resends them or uses smaller ones. This step can: a
     LOST near 1514 bytes means full-size frames do not get through.

  6. Benchmark, with BENCH only. The server sends 4 MB, then client sends
     4 MB back, with no disk involved:

         Receive: 760 KB/s (5.3 s)
         Send:    950 KB/s (4.3 s)

     The time is measured in 1/50 s. Results vary by up to about 10%
     between runs, so compare several. Transfers through the network
     between the two computers also count: a lossy cable or switch shows
     up as lower and more variable figures.

  The last line sums up the errors of the receive, send and frame size
  tests. client returns 0 if everything passed, 1 otherwise.

@endnode

@node S2stat "s2stat"

  s2stat - the driver's diagnostic counters

  Usage:   s2stat [unit]

  s2stat prints the counters and state the driver keeps for one unit
  (default 0). It can run while the TCP/IP stack has the unit open, and
  it works even when the driver's task is stuck. Run it twice, a few
  seconds apart, and compare: most values count up from when the unit
  was started.

  Every line shows the name, the value in decimal and in hexadecimal, and
  sometimes an explanation. The counters come in groups:

     @{" Unit task and card setup  " link S2Unit}
     @{" Requests from the stack   " link S2Requests}
     @{" Interrupts and card state " link S2Card}
     @{" Receive                   " link S2Rx}
     @{" Receive errors            " link S2RxErr}
     @{" Transmit                  " link S2Tx}
     @{" Transmit errors           " link S2TxErr}
     @{" Receive checksums         " link S2Sums}
     @{" Timing                    " link S2Timing}

  The last two are only filled in by drivers built for diagnosis; with
  the normal driver they show a single line saying so. Counters s2stat
  does not know (from a newer driver) are listed under "Other".

  On a healthy unit all error counters stay at 0, except TxCollisions,
  which is normal on a half-duplex network.

@endnode

@node S2Unit "s2stat: Unit task and card setup"

  Unit task and card setup

  TaskState   What the unit's task is doing right now:
                0  waiting       nothing to do (the normal state)
                1  handling requests from the TCP/IP stack
                2  servicing card, after an interrupt
                3  reading ring, taking received packets off the card
                4  sending packet, copying a packet to the card
                5  overflow recovery, after the card ran full
              A unit that stays in a state other than 0 is stuck there.

  TaskPri     Priority of the unit's task, see @{"The driver" link Driver}.

  MMUInhibit  1 if mmu.library reports the card's registers as not cached
              (normal with openpci). 0 means they could not be checked or
              are cached; the driver then flushes the data cache around
              every register access, which is slower.

  RtlConfig   The RTL8029's configuration registers, one byte each:
              $00aabbcc with aa = CONFIG0, bb = CONFIG2, cc = CONFIG3.
              $40 in CONFIG3 means the card runs full duplex.

@endnode

@node S2Requests "s2stat: Requests from the stack"

  Requests from the stack

  ReadsQueued    Read requests received: the TCP/IP stack hands the
                 driver empty requests, which received packets fill.

  WritesQueued   Packets the TCP/IP stack asked the driver to send.

@endnode

@node S2Card "s2stat: Interrupts and card state"

  Interrupts and card state

  Interrupts     Interrupts the card raised. The PCI interrupt line is
                 shared with other cards; only the card's own count.

  ServiceRuns    Times the driver's task handled the card's events.
                 Usually close to Interrupts.

  IntSeen        Every interrupt cause the card has reported, one bit
                 each:
                   $01 packet received      $10 receive ring full
                   $02 packet sent          $20 error tallies half full
                   $04 receive error        $40 data transfer complete
                   $08 send error           $80 card reset (stopped)

  LastIntStatus  The interrupt causes the last time there were any, with
                 the same bits.

  NextPage       Where the driver reads the next packet from the card's
  LastCurrent    receive ring, and where the card writes the next one
                 (as last read). Both are 256-byte pages from $4C to $7F;
                 when they are equal the ring is empty.

  TxPending      Packets on the card waiting to be sent or being sent,
                 0 to 2.

@endnode

@node S2Rx "s2stat: Receive"

  Receive

  RxFrames       Packets taken from the card.

  RxDelivered    Packets passed on to the TCP/IP stack.

  RxNoReader     Packets dropped because the stack had not asked for that
                 kind of packet, for example IPv6 on an IPv4-only setup.
                 Normal, as long as the useful traffic gets through.

  LastDropType   The kind (Ethernet type) of the last packet dropped that
                 way: $0800 IPv4, $0806 ARP, $86DD IPv6.

  RxWrapped      Packets the card stored across the end of its receive
                 ring, continuing at the start. Normal.

@endnode

@node S2RxErr "s2stat: Receive errors"

  Receive errors

  Overflows      Times the card's receive ring ran full. Packets that
                 arrive while it is full are lost. The driver's task did
                 not get the CPU in time: see the task priority in
                 @{"The driver" link Driver}.

  RxMissed       Packets the card could not store (ring full, or the card
                 stopped during recovery).

  RxCrcErrors    Packets damaged on the wire, dropped by the card: look at
                 the cable, the switch port and the duplex settings.

  RxAlignErrors  Packets with a broken length on the wire, dropped by the
                 card. Same causes as CRC errors.

  RxErrInts      Times the card reported a receive error.

  LastRxStatus   The card's receive status after the last of those:
                   $01 received fine        $10 missed (ring full)
                   $02 CRC error            $20 multicast/broadcast
                   $04 alignment error      $40 receiver disabled
                   $08 FIFO overrun         $80 deferring

  RxBadHeader    Corrupt packet headers in the card's ring. The driver
                 then throws away the ring's contents. Should never
                 happen; please report it.

  LastBadHeader  The last such header: status, next page and length,
                 one byte each from the right ($LLLLNNSS).

@endnode

@node S2Tx "s2stat: Transmit"

  Transmit

  TxStarted      Packets copied to the card for sending.

  TxDone         Packets the card reports as sent.

  TxWireIdle     Times the card finished sending with no next packet
                 ready, so the cable went quiet for a moment.

  TxIdleMsgWait  The part of those where the stack had already handed
                 over the next packet but the driver had not copied it to
                 the card yet. High values mean the driver's task was
                 kept from running.

  TxWaitPolls    Status reads spent waiting for the card to accept
                 copied packets, in total. Grows with TxStarted.

@endnode

@node S2TxErr "s2stat: Transmit errors"

  Transmit errors

  TxErrors       Packets the card failed to send.

  TxCollisions   Collisions while sending. The card retries, so these are
                 not lost. Normal on half-duplex networks.

  TxLateColl     Collisions late in a packet. A typical sign of a duplex
                 mismatch: the switch port runs full duplex, the card
                 half duplex. Set the port to auto or half duplex.

  TxCarrierLost  Packets during which the link was lost: look at the
                 cable.

  TxNoPending    Send interrupts with no packet being sent. Should stay 0.

  TxDmaTimeout   Times the card did not confirm a copied packet. Should
                 stay 0.

@endnode

@node S2Sums "s2stat: Receive checksums"

  Receive checksums

  These are only counted by a driver built with RX_VERIFY_CHECKSUMS set
  to 1 in the source, for diagnosis. It then checks the IP, ICMP, UDP and
  TCP checksums of every received IPv4 packet.

  RxSumChecked   Packets checked.

  RxSumBad       Packets that failed: they were damaged while being read
                 from the card (the card already checked them on the
                 wire).

  RxSumBadWrap   The part of those that were stored across the end of
                 the card's ring.

  LastSumBad     The last damaged packet: its ring page and length
                 ($PPPPLLLL).

  RxEchoReq      Pings received, to compare with the number sent.

@endnode

@node S2Timing "s2stat: Timing"

  Timing

  These are only measured by a driver built with "make PERF=1", for
  diagnosis. Measuring costs time on every packet, so that driver is
  slower than the normal one.

  EClockFreq      The unit of the Ticks values: E-clock ticks per second.

  TicksService    Time the driver's task spent handling the card. Behind
  TicksRxRead     each Ticks value s2stat prints the total in ms and the
  TicksRxCopy     average per received packet. RxRead is reading packets
                  off the card, RxCopy handing them to the stack.

  TicksTx         Time spent preparing packets for sending, with the
  TicksTxCopy     average per sent packet: TxCopy is the stack copying
  TicksTxUpload   the packet to the driver, TxUpload the driver copying
  TicksTxWait     it to the card, TxWait waiting for the card to confirm.

  TicksIntLat     Time from the card's interrupt until the driver's task
                  got to it, with the average per interrupt.

  MaxTicksIntLat  The longest of those waits, in microseconds.
  SlowIntLat      How many of them took more than 2 ms.

  MaxTicksTurn    The longest time from passing a received packet to the
  SlowTurn        stack to the stack's next send request, and how often
                  it took more than 5 ms.

@endnode

